      "output": "**Water (H₂O)** is a simple yet remarkably versatile chemical compound that is essential for life and plays a central role in Earth’s physical and biological systems. Here’s a concise overview:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical Formula** | H₂O – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular Structure** | Bent shape (≈104.5° bond angle) giving it a polar character. |\n| **States of Matter** | Solid (ice), liquid (water), gas (vapor). Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing) and 100 °C (boiling) under standard atmospheric pressure. |\n| **Key Physical Properties** | • **High specific heat** – absorbs heat without large temperature changes, moderating climates. <br>• **High surface tension** – allows capillary action and supports life in small organisms. <br>• **Universal solvent** – dissolves many substances, enabling biochemical reactions. <br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating aquatic life in winter. |\n| **Biological Importance** | • Constitutes ~70 % of the human body and most other organisms. <br>• Medium for metabolic reactions, nutrient transport, and waste removal. <br>• Regulates temperature through sweating and respiration. |\n| **Environmental Role** | • Drives the hydrologic cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation). <br>• Shapes geology via weathering, erosion, and sediment transport. <br>• Supports ecosystems from oceans to rainforests. |\n| **Human Uses** | • Drinking, sanitation, agriculture, industry, energy (steam turbines, cooling). <br>• Cultural and spiritual significance in many societies. |\n| **Unique Features** | • **Polarity** allows hydrogen bonding, giving water its high boiling point relative to other small molecules. <br>• **Self‑ionization**: a tiny fraction of water molecules dissociate into H⁺ and OH⁻, giving it a neutral pH of 7 at 25 °C. |\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule that, through its unique physical and chemical properties, sustains life, shapes the planet, and underpins countless natural and human-made processes."
